---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { EnergyCostCalculator } from "../../../components/electrical/EnergyCostCalculator.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/electrical/energy-cost")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Estimate the energy consumed (in kWh) and its approximate cost, based on a device's power, how long it runs, and your electricity rate."
>
  <EnergyCostCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>Formula</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>kWh = (Watts × Hours) ÷ 1000</code></li>
      <li><code>Cost = kWh × your rate per kWh</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 1,500W heater run for 4 hours: kWh = (1,500 × 4) ÷ 1000 = 6 kWh. At a rate of $0.15/kWh, estimated cost = 6
      × 0.15 = $0.90.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You enter your own electricity rate and currency symbol — this tool does not look up or assume any specific tariff.</li>
      <li>The estimate excludes taxes, fixed/standing charges, tiered pricing, and time-of-use rates unless you've already folded those into the rate you entered.</li>
      <li>This assumes constant power draw for the full duration entered; many real devices (e.g. appliances with compressors or thermostats) draw variable power over time, which this simple model doesn't capture.</li>
      <li>Results do not determine safe wiring, breaker sizing, or electrical code compliance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reference</h2>
    <p>The kilowatt-hour (kWh) is a non-SI unit accepted for use with the International System of Units, as recognized by NIST and used by utilities worldwide for billing energy consumption.</p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
